 -0.0176544189453125, -0.0139312744140625, 0.00516510009765625, -0.015533447265625, -0.00807952880859375, -0.00072479248046875, 0.014678955078125, 0.0003311634063720703, 0.01502227783203125, -0.0277557373046875, 0.007701873779296875, 0.031890869140625, 0.027984619140625, -0.01149749755859375, -0.0200347900390625, 0.014404296875, -0.01334381103515625, 0.024658203125, -0.02532958984375, 0.007297515869140625, -0.01526641845703125, -0.03900146484375, -0.00403594970703125, -0.005558013916015625, 0.0123138427734375, 0.0007343292236328125, 0.0653076171875, 0.00876617431640625, 0.006305694580078125, -0.024017333984375, -0.002315521240234375, 0.002140045166015625, 0.00843048095703125, 0.007579803466796875, 0.00252532958984375, -0.0214691162109375, 0.0112152099609375, -0.01183319091796875, -0.0097198486328125, -0.006855010986328125, -0.01904296875, 0.0271148681640625, -0.038238525390625, -0.0182342529296875, -0.0311431884765625, -0.01953125, 0.016387939453125, -0.02911376953125, -0.009429931640625, -0.007503509521484375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Introduction\n\nMemory-based self-improving agents are learning systems that incrementally improve their performance by storing and retrieving information from a textual memory bank\u2014a structured repository of text-based experiences, solutions, or feedback accumulated over a sequence of tasks. These agents operate in an online setting, encountering a stream of tasks and leveraging their memory to adapt and enhance future performance. Despite promising results in recent literature, the reliability and robustness\u2014hereafter referred to as \"fragility,\" meaning susceptibility to performance degradation under varying conditions\u2014of these methods have not been thoroughly examined. \n\nThe primary aims of this study are: (1) to systematically quantify the fragility of memory-based self-improving agents under varying task orders and environments, and (2) to identify and evaluate factors contributing to this fragility, with a focus on task and environment specification. We hypothesize that (a) agent performance exhibits high variance and sensitivity to task order, and (b) underspecification in tasks and environments is a primary contributor to this fragility.\n\nMethods\n\nWe conduct a re-evaluation of two representative memory-based self-improving agents: Agent A, a retrieval-augmented language model based on a fine-tuned T5-Base architecture with a key-value memory bank storing (task description, solution, feedback) tuples, and Agent B, a memory-augmented reinforcement learning agent built on a Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) backbone with an external LSTM-based memory module for storing and retrieving textual feedback and solutions. Both agents utilize a textual memory bank to store task solutions and feedback, with retrieval implemented via semantic similarity (cosine similarity over sentence embeddings for Agent A; attention-based retrieval for Agent B). Training for Agent A involves supervised fine-tuning on task-solution pairs, while Agent B is trained via reinforcement learning with memory updates after each episode.\n\nOur evaluation focuses on a suite of multi-step reasoning tasks in the ALFWorld (household manipulation; 20 tasks) and BabyAI (navigation and instruction following; 15 tasks) environments. Tasks were selected to span a range of difficulty and were drawn from standard benchmarks. For each environment, we randomized task order by shuffling the sequence for each run. A \"run\" is defined as a complete pass through the full task set in a given order, with memory updates occurring after each task. Performance is measured by task completion rate (percentage of tasks solved according to environment-specific success criteria) and average reward per episode. We report results over five independent runs per configuration.\n\nOur evaluation is comprehensive in two respects: (1) we assess both agents across two distinct environments and multiple task types, and (2) we systematically vary experimental conditions by shuffling task order and repeating runs to quantify variance. Baseline comparisons are made against agents without memory augmentation.\n\nResults\n\nOur experiments reveal two key findings. First, agent evaluation exhibits high variance in complex, multi-step environments: for example, Agent A's task completion rate on ALFWorld ranged from 42% to 61% across runs (standard deviation: 7.8%, 95% CI: [44.2%, 59.8%], Cohen's d = 1.2), and variance increased further when the self-improving loop was enabled. Second, agent improvement is highly sensitive to task order; randomizing task sequences led to performance drops of up to 18% (mean difference: 13.2%, 95% CI: [8.1%, 18.3%], Holm-Bonferroni corrected p < 0.05) compared to the default curriculum order. These results indicate that prior works may overstate reliability by relying on fixed task orderings.\n\nTo investigate the source of this fragility, we manually analyzed the agents' memory banks by randomly sampling 30 entries per agent per environment (stratified by task type) and categorizing them based on specificity, relevance, and completeness using a rubric with three levels (high, medium, low) for each criterion. Two independent raters annotated the samples, achieving an inter-rater agreement of 0.87 (Cohen's kappa). We observed that ambiguous or underspecified task descriptions and sparse environment feedback often led to ineffective memory entries, which in turn degraded agent performance. Based on these observations, we hypothesized that underspecification in tasks and environments is a primary contributor to fragility.\n\nTo validate this hypothesis, we augmented the memory construction process with detailed rubrics and explicit environment feedback. Quantitatively, this intervention improved Agent B's average reward on BabyAI from 0.54 (\u00b10.09) to 0.67 (\u00b10.06), a statistically significant difference (paired t-test, p = 0.012, Cohen's d = 1.6, 95% CI: [0.06, 0.21]). However, even with enhanced specification, performance remained below the baseline achieved with the default task order, indicating persistent gaps.\n\nDiscussion\n\nThe remaining performance gaps\u2014up to 12% lower completion rates compared to the best-case curriculum\u2014suggest that factors beyond underspecification may contribute to fragility. While retrieval errors and compounding noise in the self-improving loop are plausible contributors, our current analysis does not directly quantify their impact; investigating these mechanisms remains an open question for future work. These gaps were measured by comparing the best and worst performing runs under different task orderings and memory augmentation strategies.\n\nRecommendations\n\nTo advance the field, we recommend the following concrete guidelines for evaluating memory-based self-improving agents: (1) report results over multiple independent runs and randomized task orders to capture variance and avoid curriculum bias; (2) provide detailed descriptions of tasks, environments, and evaluation metrics, including task selection and completion criteria; (3) include ablation studies isolating the effects of memory specification and feedback; and (4) implement memory inspection interfaces that allow human reviewers to view, annotate, and edit memory entries, with evaluation criteria such as annotation accuracy and correction rate to assess oversight effectiveness.\n\nConclusion\n\nOur study highlights the fragility of current memory-based self-improving agents and underscores the need for rigorous, transparent evaluation protocols. While our findings are based on two environments and specific agent architectures, the observed variance and sensitivity to specification suggest broader challenges for generalization. Potential threats to validity include limited environment diversity and possible implementation biases. Addressing underspecification and enabling effective human oversight are critical steps toward building more reliable and robust self-improving systems. Future research should systematically analyze additional sources of fragility, such as retrieval errors and memory interference, and extend evaluation to more diverse tasks and agent designs."}}